(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, has taken a very strict stance in enforcing the law against overweight transport traders, which is one of the causes of road damage in Cambodia. He asserted that the law does not discriminate between the rich, the poor, the powerful and the powerless, that all overweight vehicles must be prosecuted.
The firm position to get rid of overweight trucks on this road took place on Sunday, October 14, 2018, recently, when Senior Minister Sun Chanthol led a team to inspect the repair of national roads. No. 48 (connecting National Road No. 4 to Koh Kong Province), which was damaged by some overcrowding and partly due to flooding.
The Minister of Public Works and Transport said that overweight trucks, the cars of the powerful, the powerless, the rich and the poor, all damaged the road. He said transport officials of the Ministry of Public Works would be very strict, understanding, without intervention or fear of threats from influential and powerful people.
The minister, who is building prominence in his work, added: "We do not allow discrimination between small carriers or large carriers who violate the law must be treated equally. "Our law is not made for the small, the big, the rich, the poor, the law must be respected all over our country."
Many of Cambodia's main national roads, borrowed by foreign investors at hundreds of millions of dollars per line, are seen as fragile. The damage was largely due to overcrowding. The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has also called for the strictest and most incomprehensible law enforcement regulations on all overweight transport groups.
According to the overweight calculation formula, one overweight vehicle is equivalent to 1,000 vehicles crossing the road, and if one overweight vehicle is up to 10 tons, it is equivalent to 10,000 vehicles driving. On that road.
To prevent overcrowding, 25 weighing stations are currently deployed on major national roads for weighing large trucks to prevent overweight. In addition, the mobile weighing team led by Mr. Seng Chhuon, the head of the overweight vehicle inspection team to weigh the trucks on the road.
Several overweight trucks were raided and fined under the Overweight Law. However, it was seen that despite the fine, the overweight transport did not decrease. Overweight truck seizures report by the Ministry of Public Works From month to month, the seizure of overweight trucks is constantly increasing.
According to the report of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, in the first nine months of 2018, overweight trucks were fined a total of 2,029 vehicles, of which 322 vehicles were fined by the mobile weighing team. . According to the report, each truck was overweight to 5 to 30 tons or 40 tons.
While announcing strict legal action, Mr. Sun Chanthol urged all businesses to stop overcrowded roads, pity the Royal Government, which is trying to raise funds to build and repair roads to serve the national economy. The senior minister also asked the overweight traders to be ashamed of themselves for violating the law.
Mr. Sun Chanthol appealed like this: "The freight company, please feel sorry for the road and pity the Royal Government, which is trying to raise funds to build a road for us to travel well, transport goods well for our national economy to grow." Do not make a profit for yourself, think of the country, so please do not carry more than the weight of the law.
