(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, has announced that he will take unconditional action against weighing station officials who conspired with overweight traders to allow vehicles to pass without crossing.
A warning to take legal action against the truck weighing officials in conspiracy with overweight traders was made recently on Sunday, October 14, when Senior Minister Sun Chanthol led a team to inspect the road repair. National Road No. 48 (connecting National Road No. 4 to Koh Kong Province), which was damaged due to some overcrowding and partly due to flooding.
Mr. Sun Chanthol said he had received information that officials at some weighing stations had allowed trucks to travel without crossing the scales in exchange for benefits from unscrupulous traders.
He recalled that there were about 14 weighing station officials who had been removed by him for breaking the scales and that their cases had been sent to court. The minister warned other station officials to do their job properly or he would take legal action without exception.
"Please call again and again, not that we do not follow the law," he said. Officials of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport are not allowed to destroy their own roads. "It can not be done."
Minister Sun Chanthol has vowed to work hard to prevent his officials at the weighbridge from allowing vehicles to pass without weighing them properly.
