Minister of Agriculture urges Vietnam to take strict measures on trade control, non-timber forest products and illegal cross-border fishing
(VOVworld) - Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries Veng Sakhon on October 12, 2018 called on Vietnam to take strict measures to control the trade in illegal non-timber forest products and illegal cross-border fishing. Permitted and unreported, as Cambodia is in the process of drafting regulations that meet EU requirements.
This request was made on the occasion of Minister of Agriculture Veng Sakhon and a high-level delegation of the Ministry of Agriculture meeting with Mr. Nguyen Suon Koeung, Minister of Agriculture and Rural Development of Vietnam on the morning of October 12, 2018. This.
On that occasion, Minister Veng Sakhon thanked the Vietnamese government and private sector for collecting agricultural products from Cambodian farmers such as rubber, cassava, cashew nuts and rice. He said that recently, Cambodian farmers were happy with the price of cashew and cassava, and he hoped that the Vietnamese side would continue to balance the price and quantity of agricultural products from Cambodia.
Regarding the cashew varieties donated by the Vietnam Cashew Association to Cambodia, the Cambodian Minister of Agriculture requested the Vietnamese Ministry of Agriculture to provide sanitary and phytosanitary certificates and other relevant certificates to avoid any problems that may affect the results. Farmer benefits.
At the same time, Minister Veng Sakhon also asked the Vietnamese side to help examine the possibility of increasing the price of rubber collected from Cambodia to facilitate the livelihood of Cambodian farmers. Regarding the movement of cross-border animals, the Minister asked the Vietnamese side to strengthen the prevention and control of epidemics to avoid risks between the two countries.
In addition, the Minister requested the Vietnamese side to take more stringent measures related to the management of trade in illegal non-timber forest products and illegal cross-border fishing without a report and as there is no report because Cambodia is in the process of preparing the standard letter. In accordance with the requirements of the European Union.
In response, Nguyen Suon Koeung promised to work with the Vietnamese private sector to examine the possibility of procuring rubber from Cambodia at a reasonable price.
As for cassava, Vietnam is currently facing infectious diseases, and the Ministry and experts are taking measures to eradicate infectious diseases and repair the damage and use new varieties that are resistant to diseases. Vietnamese private sector continues to keep cassava prices stable in Cambodia
The Vietnamese side is paying close attention to cross-border fishing and cross-border sub-forest product transport, and the Vietnamese government is pushing for commercial and environmental reforestation.
Among other things, he called on the two sides to set up expert-level working groups to continue discussing and resolving issues to boost bilateral cooperation and expand agricultural trade between the two countries.
