Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng: Cambodia does not allow trade in wildlife products and is not a country in need of wildlife products
LONDON (Reuters) - Cambodia is not a country that allows wildlife products to be traded, said Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Interior Sar Kheng at the Fourth Illegal Wildlife Trade Conference in London, United Kingdom. It is not legal and it is not a country that needs wildlife products for daily use.
Speaking at the conference on October 12, 2018, which was attended by more than 80 countries, including the President, Prime Minister, Ministers and senior officials of civil society and the private sector, more than 1,000 people, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng also stressed. Also, the evolution of the current global situation has also pushed Cambodia into a transit country for criminals in the transportation and trafficking of illegal wildlife products.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng stated that the crime of illegal wildlife trafficking not only brings disaster to the sustainability of the environment and natural resources of the planet, but the funds received from the crime also lead to the creation of problems. Many complex in society and the world, especially other related crimes such as money laundering, terrorism, human trafficking, drug trafficking, illegal arms trafficking, etc.
In the spirit of contributing to the 4th Conference on Illegal Wildlife Trafficking, on behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng expressed his commitment to continue to implement some important principles and measures that the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Third Conference Came out as follows:
First, continue to improve the existing legal documents through the amendment and creation of new legal documents as needed to serve the policy of protection and management of wildlife conservation more effectively. Refers to the practical response to the global evolving situation of natural resource management.
Second, continue to strengthen law enforcement by restricting the punishment or punishment for illegal activities related to illegal wildlife trafficking.
Third, cooperate and mobilize the participation of all relevant components, both national and international, as well as government institutions, private institutions, civil society organizations, relevant national-international partners, etc. To participate in and mobilize support in the fight against illegal wildlife trafficking in Cambodia, regionally and globally.
Fourth, continue to raise awareness through education to guide the general public to understand the value and importance of natural resources and wildlife, which are essential components of the ecosystem to work together to maximize the needs of the wildlife market. Such as eating, daily consumption and trade.
And 5: Continue to establish close relations and cooperation with law enforcement agencies to share experiences and information in operations to prevent and suppress transboundary wildlife trafficking in all forms and to support each other on strengthening The ability of law enforcement forces to be more effective.
