(Siem Reap): After more than 20 years of study life as well as experience in archeology and research with national and international institutions, Mr. Ear Darith, who is currently the Deputy Director of the International Center for Research and Documentation of Angkor of APSARA Authority, was CORNELL University of the United States invites to co-teach with Professor Kaja McGowan for one semester from August 1 to December 24, 2018.
The subjects taught there are "Water: Art and Politics in South and Southeast Asia." The main content of the lesson is to show the water management policy of Angkor civilization and the importance of water in religious beliefs, especially Amret water in the myth of "Ko Samuth Tuek".
He also briefly taught Khmer history from the Phnom Kingdom period to the present day, Khmer pottery, Angkor World Heritage Registration and Angkor Heritage Management by the Apsara Authority. In addition, he also participated in promoting the learning of the Khmer language and called on international students to be interested in Cambodia, both in terms of archeology and history.
In his nearly 20 years as an official of the APSARA Authority, Mr. A Darith said that this is the first time that the APSARA Authority has sent professional officers to teach international students abroad for a long time.
He said that it is a great honor for him as well as the APSARA Authority to share 25 years of experience in Angkor heritage management to international students in the United States, especially he was happy to promote Khmer culture and civilization to Students at CORNELL University and hope to have other APSARA Authority officials to share their knowledge with more international students in the future.
He has been working at APSARA Authority since 2000 and is a Professor at the Royal University of Fine Arts, Faculty of Archeology on the history of Khmer pottery. In addition, he is a professor of Khmer history and world civilization at Pannasastra University in Siem Reap.
