Reports show that internet charges have dropped significantly over the past three years.
Wen Quy, director of the Information and Telecommunication Development Department of the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology of China, said: "Since China launched a nationwide campaign three years ago to make online data transmission faster and to reduce Internet charges."
According to statistics from the International Telecommunication Union (ITU), the average data download speed of high-speed internet in China in July ranked 19th out of 133 countries around the world, and mobile data download speeds ranked 37th.
In China, she said, switching to fiber optic cables for data transmission is a popular option. About 87 percent of all households in China have access to fiber-optic Internet access. And 82% of these families are using high speed internet.
China has achieved its goal of developing its nationwide telecommunications system two years ahead of schedule, achieving both the number of households using high-speed Internet and the number of 4G Internet users.
This goal is stated in the 13th Five-Year Plan for China's National Social and Economic Development to be implemented from 2016 to 2020.
The range of cable internet service providers and the level of 4G internet usage in China are among the top countries in the world. This shows China's commitment to establish communication networks for people and businesses to use throughout the country. (China's CCTV)
