Samdech Techo Hun Sen Visits Yokohama International Port
(VOVworld) - The Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his delegation visited Yokohama International Port in Yokohama, Kanagawa Prefecture on the morning of October 7, 2018. A deep water of Japan. In cooperation with Cambodia, Japan is helping to develop the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port through the establishment of a customs system and other infrastructure.
During his visit to Yokohama Port, the Prime Minister paid close attention to the introduction of the port chain and infrastructure, especially the cooperation between the management of Yukohama International Port and the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port. Because Japan is an island nation, almost 99% of all goods are exported and imported.
Yokohama International Port is a major strategic port in Japan with the second largest container handling area in Japan, especially for navigation. Yokohama has three international ports, including one of the deepest ports. Yokohama Container Port was established in 1995 with an area of more than 320,000 hectares, which is responsible for the storage and export of Japan's main imports.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen praised the management of Yokohama International Port for allowing him and his delegation to visit.
The Prime Minister also requested the management of Yokohama International Port to continue to pay attention and increase cooperation with the management of Sihanoukville Autonomous Port in order to turn Sihanoukville Autonomous Port into a qualified port. International standards and will compete with the ports of regional and international countries.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Visits a Sewage Treatment Center in Tokyo
At the Sewage Cleaning Center in Tokyo, the presence of the Prime Minister's warm welcome was warmly welcomed by the management of the Sewerage Center.
Samdech Techo and his delegation listened intently to the presentation by the leaders of the Center on Water Production Project in Tokyo, especially the treatment of wastewater to serve the daily needs of the people in Tokyo.
With the presentation, the management of the center also informed Samdech Techo that the center is cooperating with Cambodia to bring this modern technology to Cambodia to treat some wastewater to Use for the needs and consumption of the Cambodian people, especially to reduce the health risks of the Cambodian people.
The crime of "robbery" was once covered in Article 34 of the provisions of the judiciary, criminal law and criminal procedure to be applied in Cambodia during the transition period, the period after the Paris Agreement or the UNTAC era, commonly referred to as "criminal law". "Transition" which has been implemented since 1992. The definition of robbery in the Transitional Penal Code above defines:
1. This theft took place before, during or after the use of violence. 2: This theft was committed by hacking.
In summary, since the 2009 Penal Code came into force until now, there is no more "robbery" crime, only "theft" crimes as mentioned above, so please stop There is further misunderstanding and misuse of the above crime names.
According to the rules and regulations of Preah Samma Samput, the people believe that all the meritorious ancestors or relatives who have sinned while alive, at the time of death do not know what nationality or sin is. Born as ghosts will come to receive this merit from them when these ghosts are released for 15 days.
For 15 days, all the monks were seeking the dedication of relatives at all pagodas. If, during the seven days, they visit seven pagodas and still do not find any relatives or children to cook for the monks at the pagoda to dedicate to them, these ghosts will curse various prophecies.
It is believed that all the ghosts are afraid of the sun and some poor ghosts without clothes will not dare to come out to receive benefits from their children openly. From 2am to 5am. In addition, all Buddhists in Cambodia must make food and cakes to give to the monks who wait for the quarter in the pagoda to bring blessings to the dead as well.
In order to receive blessings from the dead, such as parents or other benefactors, all the descendants of the ancestors at least once a year of this ritual must go to the pagoda once or to throw Bai Ben to each pagoda As a constraint.
