(Tokyo, Japan): The Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen and a high-level delegation of the Royal Government of Cambodia arrived safely in Tokyo, Japan at 5:40 pm in Tokyo, Japan. : 40 minutes in Phnom Penh, Cambodia.
The arrival of Samdech Techo Prime Minister and the high-level delegation was warmly welcomed by Mr. Suzuki Norikasu, Deputy Minister of Foreign Affairs of Japan, Mr. Horino Shihidehisa, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia, Mr. Ung Rachana, Cambodian Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary to Japan, as well as many senior Japanese government and Cambodian diplomats in Japan.
It should be noted that Samdech Techo Hun Sen led a delegation to fly out of Cambodia this morning to attend the 10th Mekong-Japan Summit in Tokyo, Japan.
According to the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the 10th Mekong-Japan Summit will be held from October 6 to 10, 2018. Accompanying Samdech Techo Hun Sen were Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs, as well as senior government officials and business leaders of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, during the summit, the leaders attending the Mekong-Japan Summit will also bow to the Emperor of Japan.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs also said that the 10th Mekong-Japan Summit will discuss and adopt a new Tokyo strategy aimed at improving connectivity, promoting people's well-being and protecting the environment. The strategy focuses on sustainable development and bridging the development gap between the Mekong and other ASEAN members.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen will deliver a speech focusing on the future direction of Mekong-Japan cooperation.
Mekong leaders will have a working breakfast hosted by the Japan-Mekong Parliamentary Friendship Association, a working lunch hosted by a business group and participate in a Japan-Mekong Investment Forum organized by the Japan External Trade Organization (JETRO). On that occasion, Samdech Techo will highlight the key to attracting Japanese investors to do business in the Mekong region.
On the sidelines of the 10th Mekong-Japan Summit, the Prime Minister will hold bilateral talks with Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe on various issues of common concern of bilateral, regional and international cooperation. The two prime ministers will then hold a joint press conference.
The Prime Minister will also take the opportunity to meet with the Cambodian community and students living and studying in Japan.
(Phnom Penh): Mine action continues to strengthen and promote economic development and economic growth to help reduce poverty in Cambodia. The Royal Government of Cambodia has set out a strategy towards a landmine-free Cambodia by 2025. To achieve this goal, the Royal Government of Cambodia and stakeholders must continue to work closely together on the basis of high commitment.
As of December 2017, about 1,700 square kilometers have been cleared as a result of the efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia, with the support of international donors.
Landmines and explosive remnants of war in Cambodia were the result of internal and regional conflicts from the mid-1960s to the late 1998s and to the present. According to the National Impact Assessment in 2002, 6,416 villages (46% of all villages in Cambodia) with an area of 4,544 square kilometers were affected.
There were 64,720 victims, both military and civilian, who hampered the country's socio-economic development.
The Mine Authority and the NPA have been long-term partners in capacity building since 2007 through the establishment of the National Database (DBU), including technical support, consulting and funding.
The implementation of the Capacity Development Project through the UK Department for International Development (DFID) Phase 1 began in 2014 and was completed in March 2018, and the second phase will continue from July 2018 until March 2020. This collaboration has been noted as a significant contribution to enhancing the capacity of mine authority officials and mine action planning units to better lead, manage and coordinate mine action.
The new project focuses on: First, the planning and prioritization process; Second, the development of guidelines on gender mainstreaming in mine action; third: continuing to improve the quality management system; fourth: continuing to improve. The fifth information management system: the development of management strategies to strengthen the national capacity to solve the remaining problems after 2025.
