Two private companies in Cambodia cooperate to teach animal husbandry techniques, farming and promise to buy from farmers at affordable prices
(Phnom Penh): Farmers growing crops and raising animals began to have hope after two private companies in Cambodia announced the training of animal husbandry techniques, farming techniques that make farmers spend less and get more. Both promised to buy agricultural products and buy animals from farmers at market prices to import, process, export and market.
The two major companies are: Angkor Green Investment Development Limited and Chicken Farm, and this morning, October 4, 2018, the two companies signed an agreement to "join together to support "The market is wider for Cambodian livestock farmers."
The signing ceremony was attended by Mr. Van Rithy, Executive Director of Irrigation and Angkor Gap, Mr. Than Samdy, Representative of Chicken Rice Company, as well as many distinguished guests. The signing of the agreement between the two companies is to benefit farmers and ranch owners throughout Cambodia.
Mr. Van Rithy said that the factor that led the two companies to reach an agreement was to support Cambodian farmers to produce quality and low-cost agricultural products. In which, Angkor Green has professional staff to teach and visit farmers' farms and farms regularly to guide them to grow crops and raise animals with the right techniques.
He added that Angkor Green has been supporting animal husbandry and cultivation techniques for more than seven years, while the company has been supporting the market for more than a year, but the results have been satisfactory. "The joint venture between Angkor Green and Chicken Farm will expand the scope of connectivity from producers to a larger market," he said.
He said that the company will introduce a modern marketing mechanism like never before in Cambodia, especially the system of distribution of safe pork, safe chicken, safe vegetables, fruits and safe agricultural products that are processed hygienically and quality through the distribution. Spacious, hygienic, convenient and modern, easy to buy and can order agricultural products.
He added that in the past, people did not always get the best results, and high production costs, erratic markets led to economic losses and agricultural products could not meet the quality and standards, making it difficult to find markets. Seeing this problem, the two companies provided solutions to farmers by introducing planting techniques, raising animals properly and buying from people at reasonable prices.
Mr. Than Samdy said that the cooperation with Angkor Green will lead to a very successful cooperation. As you all know, Angkor Green has been supporting farmers across the country to produce safe and low-cost agricultural products for many years.
He added that the two companies will plan to expand the scope and existing distribution network. With a plan to buy safe farm chickens from small and medium farmers, including the farming community to raise 1,000 chickens a day. As for safe Khmer pigs, we will expand the purchase from farmers to about 30 per day.
He added that as planned, "the joint venture between the two companies will support a wider market share of livestock farmers in Cambodia and also benefit small and medium farmers across the country, as well as farming communities." "In addition, farmers will be able to learn techniques in practice, and when they produce, there is a market."
