INDONESIA: The international community is mourning Indonesia's earthquake and tsunami, which killed at least 1,234 people and injured more than 600, according to a CNN report. The evening of September 29, 2018. Here is some important information about this tragedy:
The quake and tsunami affected 2.4 million people and displaced 48,000. Water seeped into the building and destroyed many homes. Communications and electricity were cut off, making rescue work even more difficult, especially for people living in remote areas.
Other colleagues were evacuated from the building before the catastrophic disaster, but he continued to work to the end to ensure the plane was able to take off safely.
* Rescue work:
Authorities have asked residents of Palu not to return to their homes and to stay away from buildings, such as fields, streets or courtyards, to prevent any accidents.
The victim was rushed to a nearby hospital while health workers were busy. In a difficult situation as electricity and communication systems were cut off and roads were destroyed, Dr. Komang Adi Sujendra called on the public to help rescue the victims. He said the victims now needed tents, medicine, cloths and a nurse.
Prior to the incident, the Indonesian Meteorological Agency had issued two tsunami warnings but canceled them after finding that the water level had receded. Indonesian President Joko Widodo has said that military forces have been deployed to the area to help with search and rescue efforts.
* History of earthquakes in Indonesia:
In 2004, the Indonesian island of Sumatra was hit by a 15-meter tsunami following a 9.1-magnitude earthquake in the Indian Ocean. A total of more than 220,000 lives were lost in countries around the region, but Indonesia was the worst hit, with 168,000 dead.
