Construction workers from 2 provinces compete in the qualifying round of SCG Construction
(Svay Rieng): Sixteen builders from Prey Veng and Svay Rieng provinces participated in the regional qualifiers of SCG Construction Company (SCG) recently, in which the winners Eligible for the national competition, which will be held later this year.
Candidates are divided into 8 groups, each group consists of two people to compete on the cement plus. The competition was held at Ieng Nget Construction Depot, the distributor of SCG in Svay Rieng.
The Construction Contractor Competition is part of the Construction Engineer Training Program initiated by SCG, one of the leading multinational corporations in ASEAN, to provide training to builders on cement and roofing work, which takes place at The two vocational training institutes in Phnom Penh are the National Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia (NPIC) and the Cambodia-Thailand Vocational Development Institute (CTSDI).
He added, "The growth of the skills of the builders combined with the new construction products and materials currently on the market, especially the quality construction products and materials from SCG will ensure that the standards and quality of construction in Cambodia will continue to grow.
All participating candidates will receive a certificate from the training program, and the winning team for the finals will receive a prize of 2 million riels and participate in an educational trip to Thailand organized by SCG. Well. The winner of the competition in Svay Rieng was Mr. Prum Pheak and Mr. Tet Kong from Prey Veng.
Mr. Prum Pheak said, “I am happy that SCG has given us the victory. I thought it was hard, but today I worked hard and won today. In the future, I will be able to win against other provinces and my work will grow, I will be able to have jobs from one place to another and help my life to be easier. "More."
SCG launched the Cambodian Builders Training Program in February this year in collaboration with the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training and developed a training program on the proper use of new construction products and materials.
Chip Mong Co., Ltd. and Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia hold training course for construction workers to improve technical and soft skills
(Phnom Penh): Chipmung Inc. Cement Corporation and the Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia (NPIC) have jointly launched a training course on repairer skills for builders and builders at the Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia to improve technical skills and competencies. Their soft.
Thirty builders and master builders, who are members of the Camel Cement Club in Phnom Penh, have started the first four-month course from October 2018 to January 2019. In addition to free admission, all participants also receive financial support for daily expenses and travel. The study takes place over the weekend and involves a shift in the workplace from Monday to Friday.
Speaking at the opening ceremony, Mr. Teang Sopheak Vichea, Representative of Chip Mong, said that this training is in line with the policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which wants to have cooperation between public and private institutions in organizing the training. Increase capacity and skills.
He stressed that "they must develop in line with the development of technical skills that are growing and in high demand. "If they have the standard construction skills, they will be able to build homes, buildings or other infrastructure with quality, sustainability and pride."
Skills that participants should learn are divided into construction skills, including bricklaying, wall plastering, site preparation, and soft skills such as problem-solving communication, teamwork, workplace safety, planning, time management, and budgeting. And so on.
In addition, Mr. Muong Phasy, Deputy Director of Studies of the National Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia, also highlighted that the Institute is pleased and pleased that the company cooperates to organize this training to increase knowledge and skills.
Meanwhile, Mr. Khon Dara, 37 years old, a master craftsman from Prek Pnov district, Phnom Penh, said that he was very happy that the company chose him to study this specialty. He said he had been a carpenter and master plasterer for 20 years, but had never attended a mortar course.
"After learning this course, I will be able to increase my work capacity and continue to train more than 20 of my apprentices to improve their skills and abilities in this lime work," he said.
Please be informed that Chipmung Inc. Cement and Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia will organize this training three times a year, where each participant must attend the theory course at school and practice on site. Today, the Camel Cement Club has 1,200 members who are carpenters and master builders from ten provinces and capitals in Cambodia.
