Most of the vehicles on the road are still illegal, in a month, 715 vehicles were inspected and 238 vehicles were found to be in violation of the law.
(Preah Vihear): Although the road traffic law has been implemented and tightened by the joint police force on the roads, it is observed that most car owners still do not obey the law, including driving recycled vehicles. Wrong technical specifications, bulky transport, overweight, no driver's license, fake driver's license, as well as vehicles without sufficient documents.
Sam Leang Try, director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Public Works and Transport, said: "Most of the roads do not obey the traffic rules, although the Preah Vihear provincial vehicle inspection team is currently inspecting the roads day and night."
The director of the provincial public works department said that the purpose of the vehicle inspection team to regularly inspect the roads is to ensure that drivers obey the traffic laws, not to get money from the fines. No.
He said that if we all respect the traffic law, it will really make us happy for ourselves, our families as well as others. But if we do not respect the traffic law, the authorities will impose fines according to the law, causing us to lose money, sometimes even affecting daily life.
At the same time as Pchum Ben, Mr. Samleang Try called on all citizens to respect the traffic law, that is, every time they drive to the pagoda or to other places of entertainment, please be safe. Have a happy family reunion, make sure our fun trips do not turn into grief as family members are in danger or lose their lives in traffic accidents.
Battambang Provincial CamControl officers continued to intercept 200 kilograms of chicken meatballs and chicken bones imported from Thailand through Phnom Dey checkpoint, Sampov Loun district, officers confiscated and immediately burned
He also called on people to be careful before buying goods at the market, especially daily food, please check carefully to avoid touching the goods to damage the health of your health in the future. CamControl officers found an oil depot along Chea Sophara Street selling insufficient oil
(Phnom Penh): On September 28, 2018, officials of the General Department of CamControl continued to inspect gas stations along Chea Sophara Street, Russey Keo District. The operation found an oil depot not selling enough.
This morning, the inspection of the gas station and depot was led by Mr. Nguot Somlyvong, Deputy Director of the Department, in cooperation with the professional officers of the laboratory.
Mr. Nguot Somlyvong said that the inspection was aimed at protecting the economic interests of consumers, protecting the machinery of all types of vehicles and protecting the environment arising from improper fuel consumption, and especially to prevent fraud.
Mr. Nguon Somlyvong said that the quality and quantity control is based on on-site monitoring equipment, such as a caliper tank with a specific size for measuring the amount of oil fired from the counter in different sizes with a capacity of 5 liters. 10 liters and 20 liters. The octane and cetan indexes are used in mobile laboratory vehicles.
As a result of quality control and quantity of fuel of 4 stations and depots in the above location, there are 3 stations and depots that comply with the quality and measurement, while 1 depot has a lack of fuel in accordance with the standard. Measurement.
After finding this, the inspection team asked the station owner and the depot to make a contract to adjust the vending machine according to the correct prediction through the competent authority.
Mr. Nguon Somlyvong confirmed that the fuel inspection team of General Department of CamControl will continue to inspect all stations throughout Cambodia and also called on gas station owners to cooperate with the inspection team of the General Department. Camcontrol Department in Legal Consumer Protection.
He also called on companies / depots that find improper fuel sales to be stopped and re-adjusted immediately, otherwise they will be subject to legal action. .
