China and Vietnam are neighbors, but past ties between the two countries have led to clashes. Tensions over both sides' claims to ownership of the South China Sea have sometimes sparked violent protests in Vietnam.
But despite territorial issues, economic ties such as trade, investment and tourism continue to grow normally.
Due to low labor costs, rich labor and a warm business environment, Chinese direct investment in Vietnam and trade reached an all-time high in 2017, rising 29 percent to more than 1 percent. Tens of millions of dollars a month.
Vietnam has overtaken Malaysia as China's largest trading partner in the ASEAN community.
"Vietnam recognizes the need to maintain strong economic ties with China," said Adam Mikathy, chief economist at Mekong Economic Corporation. It is a way to help Vietnam keep up with the world and become a developed country. But Vietnam does not want to rely on China. That is why Vietnam does not accept loans and opportunities offered by China like other countries. "Vietnam is more careful about this."
Ongoing political tensions have led to frequent protests in Vietnam. But this did not prevent Chinese people from visiting Vietnam.
Businesses in Vietnam want to see the two countries further strengthen economic ties. Vu Tan Loc, chairman of the Vietnam Chamber of Commerce, hopes the two countries will resolve their disputes through negotiation.
"I hope that all political tensions between Vietnam and China can be resolved through peaceful means, negotiation, based on agreement between the two ruling parties," said Vu Tan Loc, President of the Vietnam Chamber of Commerce. "The governments of both countries to protect the interests of our two countries." China invests millions of dollars in real estate in Cambodia
