Discover Cambodia's history at the United Nations
During his visit to the meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen will make a statement in front of world leaders attending the General Assembly. Samdech Techo Hun Sen's statement, which will be delivered on September 28, will focus on recent developments and key achievements of the Royal Government, as well as regional and international issues of common concern.
Taking the time for Samdech Techo Hun Sen to attend this important world event, Fresh News would like to highlight the history of Cambodia at the United Nations, summarized by Mr. Yong Chan Sophear, former Cambodian Ambassador to the United Nations. United Nations:
Cambodia is a peace-loving country and is recognized by 32 countries.
After the letter reached the Secretary-General, it took three years for Cambodia to become a member of the United Nations. The wait of up to three years due to the accession of a UN member state must first be approved by the UN Security Council and then by the UN Security Council. United Nations General Assembly by a two-thirds majority of all 51 member states at the time.
Please note that at that time, in addition to Cambodia, more than 10 other countries, including Japan, were considered at the same time. But at the UN Security Council, Japan was vetoed by the Soviet Union. The veto provoked pro-Japanese protests in the United States with harsh words. Until the United States says that the words of the Soviet Union and the opposition will become a historical record of the United Nations.
It is known that after the end of World War II in 1945, the Cold War between the ideology of the Soviet Union and the United States lasted until 1990. Later, under the coordination of the President of the United Nations General Assembly, Japan was removed from the list of candidates and will be discussed later. Please note that a year later, Japan became a member of the United Nations.
Cambodia became a member of the United Nations along with other countries on December 14, 1955. After becoming a full member of the United Nations, Mr. Nong Kimny, representing Cambodia, in addition to thanking the United Nations for including Cambodia as a member, also expressed disappointment with the closure of the list of candidates. That includes Cambodia.
Mr. Kimny told the United Nations General Assembly that this opposition has saddened the Cambodian people throughout the country because we know for sure that Cambodia has met sufficient conditions in accordance with the UN Charter. Nong Kimny has not yet declared Cambodia's policy of neutrality, although Cambodia attended the Bandung Conference as a member of the Non-Aligned Movement in 1955 a few months before the announcement.
Speaking as a full member of the General Assembly for the first time in September 1956, Nong Kimny reaffirmed Cambodia's neutrality, with President Norodom Sihanouk attending the 1955 Non-Aligned Movement Conference in Bandung. .
For now, the inclusion of a member state requires at least nine votes (out of 15) at the UN Security Council. Procedurally, if there is an objection or so-called veto from a superpower such as the United States, China, France, the United Kingdom or the Soviet Union, the application for membership fails. The UN General Assembly needs a two-thirds majority.
As for the suspension or expulsion from the United Nations, does any country have to experience this? In short, so far no country has been expelled.
The UN Charter allows for the suspension of a country from membership. The procedure is initiated by the UN Security Council and then submitted to the General Assembly for approval by a two-thirds majority. In the history of the United Nations, only South Africa has had its membership suspended.
According to the official website of the South African Permanent Mission in New York, South Africa was suspended in 1974 from joining the United Nations due to racist issues. Twelve years later, the country was readmitted to the United Nations.
On the issue of withdrawal in 1965, Indonesia chose to withdraw from the United Nations because it was unhappy that Malaysia had become a non-permanent member of the UN Security Council. But 18 months later, Indonesia returned to the United Nations without re-applying for membership.
As for Cambodia, throughout its 62-year history until today, the United Nations has never issued a decision to suspend or withdraw Cambodia's seat at the United Nations. It should be noted that the transition from one government to another did not result in the loss of a state seat at the United Nations.
But according to Benny Vidiano, a professional diplomat working for the United Nations during the UNTAC era, who wrote a book entitled "Dancing in the Shadow of Sihanouk, the Khmer Rouge, and the United Nations after the Genocide in Cambodia." Due to the conflict between the two Cambodian governments, the Cambodian seat at the United Nations has been left vacant twice.
For September 1997 and September 1998, no Cambodian representative addressed the United Nations General Assembly. After a new coalition government, Hun Sen was seen addressing the United Nations General Assembly in September 1999.
Please note that since Cambodia became a member of the United Nations, the Secretary-General of the United Nations has visited Cambodia three times. The first was Dek Hamaszul, who arrived in Cambodia on March 11, 1959, after President Norodom Sihanouk addressed the UN General Assembly in September 1958. The second was Boutros Boutros-Ghali in April 1993, and the third was Ban Ki-moon in October 2010.
As a member of the United Nations, Cambodia has twice served as Vice-President of the United Nations General Assembly. The first time in the 56th session of the United Nations General Assembly, 2001-2002 and the second time in the 73rd session of the United Nations General Assembly, 2018-2019. Through this role, Cambodia may have the opportunity to chair meetings of the General Assembly on behalf of the President and at the request of the President.
In addition, Cambodia will be a member of the General Committee, which functions as a review of the provisional agenda and a list of additional discussion points, recommends the General Assembly, and assists the President and the General Assembly in preparing the agenda for each plenary session. In particular, the general work of the General Assembly.
