The following is a statement from the Ministry of Culture: The US Ambassador commended Cambodia for its progress in all areas, especially the economy and agriculture.
The main purpose of this meeting is to explore investment opportunities in agriculture and, secondly, to explore the possibility of trade between the two countries, especially with potential local companies to develop agriculture.
According to a press release posted on Veng Sakhon's official Facebook page, during the meeting, Ambassador William Heidt praised and appreciated the progress made in all areas, especially the economic and agricultural sectors. The government has achieved so much in the past that it has contributed to raising living standards, reducing poverty and boosting economic growth.
As a result of the cooperation and results of the projects, the agricultural sector has continued to grow, and through the Ambassador, the Minister hopes and believes that the United States Government will continue to support the Royal Government of Cambodia in developing the country. Further growth, especially the US private sector, will invest more in agriculture in the future.
The Minister also stated that the Royal Government has now set out strategic plans and policies for the development of key infrastructure, such as roads, deep-sea ports, airports, electricity supply, etc. In particular, the Ministry has been promoting the development of human resources, agricultural diversification and agricultural mechanization to help boost the processing and packaging sector to grow agricultural exports in the future.
At the same time, both sides also raised important topics to discuss, including:
* Royal Government Agricultural Policy and Strategic Plan
* Sanitary and phytosanitary standards for domestic and international markets
* Key products with potential for export
* Processing sector and infrastructure that support this sector
* Strengthen and expand cooperation between the two countries in the field of agriculture
* Promoting small and medium enterprises
* Development of human resources in agriculture, especially the younger generation, and strengthening good relations between the private sector and the state.
