The rarest plants in the South Pole are seriously threatened!
LONDON: Climate change, one of the world's hottest problems, is claimed by scientists to be destroying ancient Antarctic moss fields. This is according to a quote from the BBC on Tuesday evening, September 25, 2018.
This claim is based on a study that took more than a decade, and the findings show that the vegetation area of East Antarctica is changing rapidly due to warmer climates.
When you hear the word "Antarctic", you can expect a spectacle of white glaciers, but in some areas there are also green plants, they are a kind of moss that emerges from below. The snow falls for about six weeks, which is one season.
While West Antarctica and the Antarctic Peninsula are among the fastest growing regions on Earth, the East Antarctic has not yet been significantly affected by global warming. Therefore, scientists did not expect to see any significant changes in the plants there. But after receiving the results of the above study, the researchers were very surprised when they saw how fast it changed.
Since 2000, scientists have observed that Antarctic mosses have changed from green to red and could turn gray if they were more severely affected. The turning to red is the result of their production of protective substances, and when they reach gray, it means they will soon die.
In addition, scientists have found evidence that changes in the East Antarctic are caused by the combined effects of climate change and the loss of the Earth's ozone layer, which can cause bad light from the sun to threaten. The South Pole of the Earth.
