Nearly 600 luxury saplings were planted by the chief abbot of Kampeng pagoda and the authorities of Sangkat Choam Chao I together in the pagoda area on the first day of Kan Ben
(Phnom Penh): 565 luxury saplings, including Kranhong, Beng, Neang, Nuon, Koki, Chheuteal and Thnong, obtained from a nursery in Peam Ro district, Prey Veng province, and some other places were planted in the compound of Wat Kampeng in Choam Chao Ty commune. 1 Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh, this morning, September 25, 2018, which is the first day of the Kan Ben festival, a traditional Buddhist ceremony.
The tree planting ceremony was also attended by Ratana Monivant Davy, Chief of Wat Kampeng, Major General Sok Sokunthea, Mr. Kang Vong, Chief of Sangkat Choam Chao 1, and the participation of Cambodian youth groups, government officials around the commune and the people. Many more citizens as well.
Rev. Ratana Monivant Davy is adamant that today is a good day to start the first Kan Ben festival in the Buddhist tradition, so he decided to plant trees in the temple today because now Buddhists come to do many festivals where he has the opportunity to participate in planting for the public good and our natural resources.
He also said that planting trees is like doing rituals, because when the trees are big, they provide shade for all of us, especially the Buddhists who come to perform rituals in this temple to feel cool and help. Make the environment and beauty in the temple better, more comfortable and comfortable for everyone.
According to Ratana Monivant Davy, he wants to plant more than 1,000 trees, but the first step is to plant 565 trees because the soil is too wet after the dry season, the pagoda will continue to plant more to make the temple This becomes a mixed-use luxury garden for all people and animals.
Mr. Kang Vong asked the village chiefs, villagers and Buddhists in the surrounding villages to take care of this tree to grow well, especially do not let cows come to eat and rub the tree because it is easy to die when it is not. The roots are strong. Ms. Men Sam An urges all doctors to take care of and treat patients without discrimination or caste discrimination
