In fact, US imports into China are relatively small compared to the volume of Chinese exports to the United States.
US President Donald Trump has announced that he will increase tariffs by 10 percent on $ 200 billion worth of Chinese imports by Sept. 24 and raise tariffs to 25 percent. On January 1, 2019, if Beijing does not make any concessions to the United States.
Imports from the United States to China total $ 150 billion. When China's policy of raising tariffs on the United States comes into force, it will have an impact on $ 110 billion worth of US imports. That means the rest of the goods are worth only $ 40 billion, which is not affected. That is why China cannot implement a strategy to raise taxes against the United States.
An opinion piece published in the People's Daily, an official spokesman for the Chinese Communist Party, claimed that China must use a strategy (dollar tax for dollars) because China does not take the US strategy against the United States. Not back.
The same source said that China can not retaliate against the United States by "quantity" should create a new strategy and war itself. Beijing may levy lower tariffs on US imports, which are rare, but instead impose higher tariffs on easily available alternatives, such as luxury goods, savings products, especially competing products. With Chinese factory directly.
Nearly 300 products imported from China have been removed, in whole or in part, from the US tariff list following a direct request from US business groups. One of the items excluded is rare earths, an indispensable resource for US high-tech companies, while China is the world's largest exporter.
Finally, a US tariff increase on Chinese imports of about $ 200 billion and a retaliatory tariff from China on US products of $ 60 billion came into force on Monday morning. The two countries have just raised tariffs on $ 50 billion worth of imports this year.
Chinese products subject to new tariffs from the United States range from vacuum cleaners to Internet equipment, while US products subject to US tariffs include natural gas and some aircraft.
