Minister of Agriculture visits 4 provinces to inspect crops and assess the damage caused by floods and droughts
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Ms. Mak Sitha, on September 22, 2018, visited Oddar Meanchey, Banteay Meanchey, Battambang and Pursat provinces to inspect the cultivation and evaluation of crops. Damage caused by floods and droughts on agricultural production.
Also present were the Secretary of State, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, the Delegation in charge of the Director of Forestry Administration and some other colleagues this morning, September 22, 2018.
The purpose of the above mission is to inspect the cultivation work and assess the impact of damage caused by floods and droughts on agricultural production, especially rice, and then examine the possibility of distributing rice seeds and crop seeds to the affected families. The Ministry of Agriculture has some inventory and some of it is sponsored by the Royal Government.
Program 1: Siem Reap and Oddar Meanchey Provinces (Restoring Rice Production) On the way to Oddar Meanchey Province, he visited farmers in Moeng Commune, Srey Snom District, Siem Reap Province to ask about the impact and the project to restore rice production on the land. 1660 hectares affected. On this occasion, Mr. Veng Sakhon donated 30 tons of rice seeds to the Provincial Department of Agriculture, including 3 tons of Srey Snom District Department of Agriculture.
From Srey Snom district, continue to Chong Kal district, Oddar Meanchey province, accompanied by the provincial governor. Mr. Veng Sakhon visited and asked farmers about the work of growing crops and the impact on the rice crop due to the drought in Pong commune, Chong Kal district.
According to the department, rice production in three provinces, Samrong, Chongkal and Banteay Ampil, affected more than 10,000 hectares and completely damaged 500 hectares. Overall, the rice growing situation has improved due to the continuous rains since the beginning of September.
Separately, in Samrong district, Bansay Reak commune, Bansay Reak village, Mr. Veng Sar Khon and his colleagues inspected modern agricultural farming, selected seeds, used agricultural machinery and applied correct techniques, in collaboration with the Cambodian Agricultural Research and Development Institute (KADI). 43 hectares of land owned by a farmer named Bou Sam Hen.
From Samrong district, continue to Banteay Ampil district, Vil commune, Ov Lok village to meet farmers whose farmland is affected but can be rehabilitated. This morning, the Minister of Agriculture also instructed the General Department of Agriculture and the Director of the Department of Agriculture of the two provinces to inspect the restoration of rice production and cultivation work this morning:
Prepare to bring enough rice seeds to distribute to farmers in a timely manner and should also be ready to plant seeds, because at the end of the transplanting season, some places can not be cultivated, should plant food crops to avoid leaving vacant land.
Mr. Veng Sakhon praised, encouraged and supported farmer Bou Samhen who applied the techniques to boost his rice production and helped train other farmers to understand and learn the techniques in rice production. To increase the yield on the same land, maintain quality, market and increase revenue.
The Minister of Agriculture stated that the Department should select more farmers like this to help disseminate techniques to increase awareness to make positive changes and achieve the strategic plan for agricultural development 2019-2023 and the vision of Samdech Techo. Hun Sen in 2030.
