The project to strengthen the network of civil society organizations for environmental development has been completed with good results.
The closing workshop was held in the presence of Mr. Ngan Chamroeun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Executive Vice President of the Secretariat of the National Committee for Sub-National Democratic Development (NCDD).
The project "Strengthening Civil Society Network for Environmental Development" aims to strengthen the network of civil society organizations in Cambodia to become a key player in improving governance and accountability and promoting Develop environmentally sound policies.
The project also aims to strengthen the institutional capacity of civil society organizations to better and more effectively formulate environmental policies in the target provinces at the sub-national level, including Prey Veng, Svay Rieng, Kampong Thom, Siem Reap, Kratie, Stung Treng, Mondulkiri. And Ratanakkiri.
Speaking on the occasion, Mr. Ngan Chamroeun emphasized that civil society organizations have played an important role in assisting the Royal Government of Cambodia for the development of sub-national administrations, so this project will help develop the capacity of civil society organizations to address challenges and strengthen resilience. And the needs of the people.
The Secretary of State added that the implementation of this project is in line with the strategic objectives of the Royal Government, which always expects that the establishment of a strong network of civil society organizations will play an important role in promoting effective communication between communities. Citizens with constructive local authorities.
At the same time, Mr. Soeung Saroeun, Director of Operational Committee for Cambodia (CCC), said that through the project "Strengthening the network of civil society organizations for environmental development" during the past 30 months, the organization has invested in a number of key programs. 5.
Cambodia's first hair research and conservation center scheduled to officially open in late October
(Siem Reap): The first hair research and conservation center in Cambodia will be established and is scheduled to be officially inaugurated at the end of October 2018.
The naming of the "Kesor Sok An Phnom Kulen Research and Conservation Center" is to commemorate the noble merits of the late Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An in the cause of nation building, protection of natural resources, conservation of biodiversity and conservation of Cambodian cultural and human heritage and tourism.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment Net Pheaktra said that the center will be a collection point for the protection, rescue and conservation of endangered and endangered forest species and further research.
He said that at present, the Sok An Phnom Kulen Hair Research and Conservation Center has been constructing the first phase and collecting wild hair from Phnom Pokulen to plant and maintain it gradually.
Secretary of State Neth Pheaktra emphasized that the purpose of establishing the Center for Research and Conservation of Wildlife is to study the wildlife species that exist in Cambodia, including rare and unidentified species, to preserve the endangered species. Restored into a natural sanctuary.
The center will play a role in educating students and the public to understand, love and inspire to participate in the care, protection and conservation of wildlife in the Kingdom of Cambodia, as well as to promote ecotourism in the Phnom Kulen. .
According to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, Cambodia can be considered a country rich in natural resources and biodiversity, of which there are more than 500 species of wild orchids, some of which are rare. Of those, only about 360 have been identified.
Kesor Kos Prey is currently under threat from various forms of trafficking to foreign countries, especially Thailand and Vietnam, but there are no records or details of this trade yet. The main endangered species According to preliminary research, about seven species of endemic species are recorded in Cambodia every year, some of which are endangered.
