Two gas stations and depots found in Dangkor and Por Sen Chey districts selling substandard and fraudulent oil
(Phnom Penh): The fuel inspection team of the General Department of CamControl of the Ministry of Commerce through a mobile fuel laboratory vehicle led by Mr. Nhung Ven, Deputy Director of the Department in cooperation with the professional officers of the laboratory on September 20, 2018. Inspect the quantity, quality and retail price of fuel at some stations and depots in Khan Dangkor and Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh.
The purpose of these inspections is to protect the economic interests of consumers, to protect the engines of all types of vehicles and to protect the environment arising from improper fuel consumption, and in particular to prevent fraud.
According to Nhung Wen, the quality and quantity control is based on on-site monitoring equipment, such as a caliper tank with a specific volume for measuring the amount of oil fired from the counter in different sizes, with a capacity of 5 liters 10 liters and 20 liters. The octane and cetan indexes are used in mobile laboratory vehicles. Retail prices at stations and depots are based on the notification of the Ministry of Commerce.
As a result of quality control and quantity of fuel of 6 stations at the above location, 4 locations sold fuel in accordance with both quantity and quality and 2 locations did not comply with both quantity and quality. For the retail price, all 6 locations have complied with the notification of the Ministry of Commerce.
The General Department of CamControl called on the people to be careful of fire safety by turning off the engine when refueling in the station and to provide information to CamControl officials in the capital-province or to the General Department of CamControl in case of any event. . HKL Shares Financial Management Experience to Clients in Cambodia Provinces
(Phnom Penh): To contribute to the well-being of the community through financial training to increase financial literacy to the community, Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Limited has been sharing its financial management experience to many clients in In some provinces and cities in Cambodia.
According to Oknha Huot Ieng Tong, CEO of Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Limited, "We not only provide financial solutions to our clients, but also focus on maintaining the well-being of the community." .
The CEO of Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Limited added that financial management knowledge has a great impact on the social well-being of clients as well as promoting their long-term growth.
"Saving money is the most important knowledge for them because they can save money for their children's education, save money for small business and many other purposes," said Duke Huot Ieng Tong.
Please be informed that the purpose of creating this program is to improve the life of the community by improving the quality of life as well as gaining knowledge of financial management. The selection of each course is made by surveying the actual needs of the community, whether it should be training on savings management or debt management.
The program started in July 2018 with more than 150 participants and aims to capture up to 300 participants by the end of the year. Most of the participants were heads of families, entrepreneurs and civil servants at the grassroots level.
HKL first established this financial management training course in 2012 in Kampong Cham province, and this program was held several times before re-launching in 2018. The Cambodian Red Cross visited and supported the first humanitarian donation 6 to Aid for Hope for the Community
