Minister of Environment Encourages Cambodian Southeast Asian Youth Ship Delegates to Share Cambodian Culture and Development on the International Stage
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, this morning, September 18, met with a delegation of Cambodian Southeast Asian Youth Ship before going to participate in the 45th Southeast Asian and Japanese Youth Ship Program in Japan and the country. The other four in ASEAN include Brunei, the Philippines, Thailand and Vietnam, with a total of 330 youths in 52 days in 2018.
On this occasion, the Minister of Environment encouraged and encouraged young people to actively participate in learning about other countries, as well as show what is the culture and progress of Cambodia today.
At the same time, the Minister mentioned the work of environmental management, environmental protection, which is the core work of the Ministry, has been paying attention to how to be effective and encourage all young people to contribute as well as help promote the environment.
The Ministry of Environment has modernized the Ministry by enhancing both human resources and capacity to participate in job development within the institution to improve and effectively participate in environmental protection.
The 45th 52nd Southeast Asian and Japanese Youth Ship will be launched on October 23, 2018 and return to Cambodia on December 12, 2018. A total of 330 ASEAN and Japanese youths will first meet in Tokyo, Japan for the official opening ceremony.
It should be reminded that the Association of Southeast Asian Youth Ships of Cambodia was established on November 7, 2004 by some former youth delegates with the support of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports. The total number of members in 2018 is 569 and the 11 member countries are ASEAN and Japan with a total of 13,703 members. This association was established for four main purposes, including:
First, an umbrella that maintains and strengthens the friendship and solidarity between all former delegates.
Second: Organize training courses before departure to share knowledge and experience from all angles to the next generation of youth delegates to participate in the program effectively and successfully.
Third, mobilize resources and inspire young people to take the initiative to create and participate in social projects more actively.
Fourth: Participate in achieving the objectives of the Southeast Asian and Japanese Youth Ship Program.
