(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Mr. Veng Sakhon, expressed his deep concern over a disease called "Mosaic" disease, which causes serious harm to cassava crops.
The Minister said that the emergence of the dangerous "mosaic" disease on cassava crops in Southeast Asia is a matter of grave concern, posing a serious threat to the entire production chain (production, processing and export). ) And can affect the national economic health of each country as well.
The concern was raised by Minister Veng Sakhon, who presided over the opening of a regional seminar on cassava mosaic disease control plan in Southeast Asia on the morning of September 18.
"The disease broke out in India and Sri Lanka and for the first time in Southeast Asia in 2015," he said. Then in recent years the disease has spread to Vietnam and Cambodia, which have been wreaking havoc on cassava production to an alarming extent. "Therefore, it is necessary to study and evaluate the epidemic situation and put in place the most effective measures to prevent the spread of the disease."
Mr. Veng Sakhon stated that cassava is one of the priority crops of Cambodia, which has been serving as the food of farmers as a raw material for the agro-industrial sector and also a crop that countries in the region are paying close attention to. Constantly developed as well. In 2017, Cambodia considered cassava as the second priority crop after rice with an area of more than 0.61 million hectares and a yield of more than 13.8 million tons.
"Cambodia has become a major supplier of cassava products, both in raw materials and finished products, in Southeast Asia," he added. In fact, more than 3.8 million tons of cassava products were exported to regional and global markets in 2017.
The Minister made some important recommendations for all stakeholders, members of the countries in the region, students and research scientists to consider in order to find the key to effectively address this issue in order to prevent the spread of the disease. :
1: Joint research to find the source of the disease, the nature of the spread and put in place measures to prevent and prevent the spread of disease from one area to another. Take immediate action to reduce or prevent disease outbreaks that could threaten cassava production.
2. All relevant institutions, sub-national authorities must fulfill their role in controlling and controlling the import of cassava seeds from countries with outbreaks, the movement of seeds from areas with outbreaks to disease-free areas, especially along the border.
3: Continue efforts to organize seed production in the provinces themselves to avoid importing seeds from areas with outbreaks.
4. Increase cooperation in research, information sharing and solutions between countries in the region.
5: Increase awareness of the disease and preventive measures to farmers, farm owners and producers widely and ask for their cooperation in the event of an outbreak.
