Sun Chanthol Shows Infrastructure Progress in Cambodia at ASEAN-South Korea Infrastructure Ministerial Meeting
(Seoul, South Korea): On Monday, September 17, 2018, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, led a delegation from the Ministry of Public Works and Transport. Cambodia attends the first ASEAN-South Korea Infrastructure Ministerial Meeting in Seoul
The meeting was chaired by Kim Hyun Mee, Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the Republic of Korea. Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said the meeting would further strengthen cooperation in infrastructure development between ASEAN countries and the Republic of Korea.
Addressing the meeting, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol highlighted the recent achievements of Cambodia in implementing the "Smart Cities" and "Sustainable Infrastructure" initiatives in the post-Industrial Revolution phase. 3 to the Fourth Industrial Revolution.
The Senior Minister highlighted the construction and development of an effective road network, freshwater ports, deep-sea ports, airports, railways connecting Phnom Penh with Thailand's railway system and deep-sea ports.
At the same time, the Senior Minister also informed the meeting about various development projects, including: the construction project of the expressway from Phnom Penh to Sihanoukville deep-water port and the construction of a new 14.5-meter-deep container port in parallel. In view of the expansion of the Asia Highway link from Thailand to Phnom Penh and the ASEAN Connectivity Master Plan 2025.
The Senior Minister also highlighted Cambodia's economic growth in the annual growth rate of around 7% under the shadow of peace and political stability of the country and promoting Cambodia from a low-income country to a middle-income country.
Finally, the Senior Minister expressed his deep gratitude and appreciation to the people and government of the Republic of Korea for their support in improving Cambodia's infrastructure and for contributing to the development of a master plan for the transportation sector. Waterways, rail networks and road safety as well.
Korean government expresses willingness to help develop infrastructure in Cambodia
(Seoul, Korea): The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the Republic of Korea, Kim Hyun Mee, said that the South Korean government will assist the Royal Government of Cambodia in developing better infrastructure.
The South Korean government expressed its support for the construction of infrastructure in Cambodia during a working meeting between Kim Hyun Mee and Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, in Seoul, Korea. September 17, 2018.
During the meeting, the Transport Ministers of the two countries discussed ways to better cooperate with each other to improve infrastructure in Cambodia through infrastructure technology.
Minister Kim Hyun Mee informed the Republic of Korea of its willingness to assist the Royal Government of Cambodia in developing systematic infrastructure. This development includes technology, national infrastructure and basic information, technology support, software research and software exchange, as well as the establishment of the Infra Technology Center in Cambodia.
The Senior Minister briefed the South Korean Minister on current and future projects related to KOICA, including: improving national roads, wastewater treatment plants in Siem Reap and Takhmao; Research Center and Institute, Laboratory and Training and Master Plan for Port Development.
Mr. Sun Chanthol asked the Government of the Republic of Korea to support these projects. In response to the Minister's request, the Minister promised to take these requests to the South Korean government.
The senior minister thanked the South Korean government for providing comprehensive infrastructure development assistance and looked forward to future cooperation between the two countries. Samdech Techo Hun Sen issues sub-decree to transfer and appoint 3 Deputy National Police Commissioners
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, has issued a sub-decree to transfer duties and appoint three police officers at the rank of General to the Deputy Commissioner of the National Police.
The three general-level police chiefs who have been appointed as Deputy National Police Chiefs are: First: Lt. Gen. In Bora, 2nd: Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun and 3rd: Lt. Gen. Ten Suon.
Lt. Gen. In Bora is currently the Chief of the Central Department of Judicial Police, Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun is currently the Chief of Staff and Lt. Gen. Ten Suon is currently the Chief of the Water Border Police Department.
Please be informed that the current Deputy Commissioner of the National Police has 17 members after Samdech Techo Hun Sen appointed 3 more Deputy Commissioners, a total of 20 Deputy Commissioners.
