Citizens can dedicate their time one morning to celebrate "World Cleanup Day" to help clean up litter on the ground, both on land and in the water, with the international community celebrating World Cleanup Day. Simultaneously.
Net Pheaktra, secretary of state and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that World Cleanup Day today focused on reducing environmental pollution and the use of plastic bags. This program is designed to inspire and encourage the participation of people from all walks of life in order to make the world a litter-free world.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment continued that Cambodia has celebrated World Cleanup Day throughout Cambodia, and some provinces have started to do so since September 14 to meet some of the people's challenges in all areas that require Take care to clean up the environment everywhere and at all times to become a clean social movement in Cambodia and around the world.
Globally, about 4 billion tons of waste is dumped on Earth, of which more than 8 million tons of plastic waste is dumped into the sea every year. Solid waste management in Cambodia is taken care of by the Royal Government of Cambodia, the Ministry of Environment and local authorities.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, stated that plastic pollution is a concern of Cambodia, which requires the participation of all parties, especially the people as consumers to prevent and reduce plastic pollution.
In the face of pollution by plastic bags, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment Net Pheaktra asked people to clean up the environment, pack properly and store garbage by properly sorting waste and reducing the use of plastic bags. The 3R principle is to reduce reuse, recycling.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that cleaning up the environment around us is necessary. If our house is clean, our village is clean, our public spaces are clean, there is no garbage, no plastic bags, that means we have contributed to building national values, promoting the prestige and honor of the Khmer nation and for our well-being, because the environment is our problem. So we have to work together for the environment.
