(Hanoi, Vietnam): The Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen sees that connectivity and legal reform are essential for the Mekong countries in the context of the current Fourth Industrial Revolution to move forward. Strengthening economic and trade relations and cooperation.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen made the remarks while attending the Plenary Summit on the New Vision for the Mekong Region hosted by the World Economic Forum in Hanoi, Vietnam on September 12, 2018. .
Responding to questions from the Plenaries of the Plenary Summit on a New Vision for the Mekong on Technology in the Context of the Fourth Industrial Revolution, the Prime Minister stated that infrastructure connectivity remains a priority to ensure that countries in the region The five Mekong countries, including Cambodia, Thailand, Laos, Vietnam and Myanmar, can connect more easily.
In addition to focusing on infrastructure connectivity, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also suggested that Mekong countries focus on streamlining the legal landscape. According to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the legal differences of each country are also barriers to trade between the five countries in the region.
The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia underlined, "We connect the physical, the digital, and more importantly, the connection to facilitate the legal aspect." "Without legal redress, the legal gap between individual countries will make integration and cooperation, even border tariff facilitation difficult."
As many as 700,000 Rohingya have reportedly fled Rakhine State to Bangladesh since the violence broke out in August 2017. UN investigators have accused the Myanmar military of committing Rohingya genocide, despite Suu Kyi's denials. Venezuelan President travels to Beijing to seek Chinese help
(Caracas): Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro is in Beijing to discuss an economic deal as his government seeks loans from China to address the country's current economic crisis. This was released by Reuters on Thursday, September 13, 2018.
Speaking to state media on Wednesday (local time) at Caracas airport, Maduro said: "I am traveling to China with high expectations and we will meet as "Again in the next few days with great results." Maduro did not provide further details, and Venezuela's information ministry and Chinese foreign ministry have not yet commented.
In July, Venezuela's finance ministry said it could receive $ 250 million in aid from the China Development Bank to address falling oil prices. Venezuela has also agreed to a $ 5 billion loan from China, but has not yet received the full amount.
